Understanding Cline, MCP, ARC-1, and SAP BTP — Explained from the Basics
This document explains how an AI coding assistant can safely connect with SAP systems, read ABAP objects, analyze code, and support development work. It is written in a simple, human-friendly way so that even someone who has never heard about Cline, MCP, ARC-1, SAP BTP, BAS, or Cloud Connector can understand the overall picture step by step.
________________________________________
Layer 0 — First, understand the main systems involved
1.	Your SAP system — This is the main business system, such as SAP S/4HANA or SAP ECC. It stores important business data, ABAP programs, tables, users, authorizations, transports, and system configuration. In simple words, this is the place where the real SAP development and business logic exist.
2.	SAP BTP — SAP Business Technology Platform is SAP’s cloud platform. You can think of it like a cloud-based extension area for SAP. Instead of directly exposing your internal SAP system to the internet, SAP BTP provides services such as Cloud Foundry applications, destinations, connectivity, SAP HANA Cloud, and AI-related services.
3.	Your workstation or SAP Business Application Studio — This is where the developer works. SAP Business Application Studio, also called BAS, is a browser-based development environment that feels similar to VS Code. It provides a place to write code, run tools, connect to SAP services, and use extensions.
Simple example: imagine your SAP system as the main factory, SAP BTP as the secure gate and delivery area outside the factory, and BAS as your desk where you control and monitor the work.
________________________________________
Layer 1 — What is a GenAI agent?
A GenAI agent is more than a normal chatbot. A normal chatbot usually answers based on the text you type. An agent can go one step further: it can decide which tool it should use, call that tool, read the result, and then continue the work. For example, if you ask, “Read program ZTEST,” the AI understands that it needs to use a tool that can read an ABAP program. It calls that tool, receives the program code, analyzes it, and then explains the result to you.
Cline is one example of such an AI agent. It works inside VS Code or SAP Business Application Studio and connects an AI model with external tools. These tools may read files, search code, validate projects, or connect to SAP systems. Other similar developer agents also exist, but the important point is that Cline gives the AI a controlled way to perform technical actions instead of only replying in chat.
________________________________________
Layer 2 — What is MCP?
MCP stands for Model Context Protocol. It is a common standard that defines how an AI agent communicates with external tools and systems. A simple way to understand MCP is to think of it like a universal adapter. Just like a USB port allows many different devices to connect to a computer, MCP allows many different tools to connect to an AI agent in a standard way.
•	Before MCP, each AI tool or coding assistant often needed its own custom integration method. This made tool integration difficult because one tool built for one assistant might not work easily with another assistant.
•	With MCP, a developer can build an MCP server once and expose useful actions through it. Any MCP-compatible AI agent can then connect to that server and use those actions in a predictable way.
An MCP server is a small program that acts like a service counter. It tells the AI agent what actions are available, accepts requests from the AI agent, performs the requested action, and sends the result back in a structured format.
1.	It announces the list of tools it supports, including the tool name, purpose, and required inputs.
2.	It runs the requested tool when the AI agent asks for it.
3.	It returns the result back to the AI agent, usually in a structured JSON format that the agent can understand.
MCP can communicate in two common ways:
•	stdio — In this method, the AI agent starts a local program on the same machine and talks to it through standard input and output. This is commonly used for local tools, such as project validators, file utilities, or local code generators.
•	streamableHttp — In this method, the MCP server runs remotely as a web endpoint. The AI agent sends requests over HTTPS and receives responses back. This is useful when the tool is hosted in the cloud, such as an SAP BTP Cloud Foundry application.
In the configuration explained later, both methods are used: remote SAP-related tools use streamable HTTP, while UI5 and CAP helper tools run locally through stdio.
________________________________________
Layer 3 — What is ARC-1?
ARC-1 is an MCP server designed for SAP ABAP-related work. In simple words, ARC-1 gives the AI agent SAP-specific abilities. Through ARC-1, the AI agent can read ABAP programs, search SAP objects, analyze where an object is used, run checks, and help understand dependencies. Instead of the AI directly entering SAP, ARC-1 acts as a controlled middle layer between the AI agent and the SAP system.
Tool	Does
SAPRead	Read any ABAP artefact (class, program, CDS view, table row)
SAPSearch	Fuzzy-search across TADIR (SAP's object registry)
SAPWrite	Create/update ABAP source (write path — usually NOT auto-approved)
SAPActivate	Activate an inactive draft
SAPNavigate	Where-used, definition, hierarchy
SAPLint / SAPDiagnose	abaplint + SAP syntax check + ATC + unit tests
SAPContext	Compressed dependency graph ("what would break if I change this?")
SAPTransport	List/create/release CTS transports
SAPManage	Package lifecycle, feature probing, FLP tiles
Behind the scenes, ARC-1 communicates with SAP using ABAP Development Tools-related APIs. These are similar to the APIs used by Eclipse ADT when an ABAP developer opens, edits, checks, or navigates SAP development objects. Because ARC-1 needs to reach the SAP system securely, SAP BTP connectivity services are used in between.
________________________________________
Layer 4 — How ARC-1 securely reaches your SAP system through SAP BTP
Most company SAP systems are protected behind a firewall. They are not openly available on the public internet, and that is a good security practice. However, an application running on SAP BTP may still need to communicate with that protected SAP system. For this, SAP provides secure connectivity services.
1.	SAP Cloud Connector — This is installed inside the company network. It creates a secure outbound tunnel from the internal network to the SAP BTP subaccount. The important point is that the company does not need to open inbound firewall access from the public internet. The Cloud Connector controls which internal systems and resources can be reached from SAP BTP.
2.	SAP BTP Destination Service — This works like an address book for cloud applications. A destination stores the target system URL, authentication method, proxy type, and other connection details. Instead of hardcoding system URLs and credentials in the application code, the application simply asks BTP for the destination details at runtime.
So the request flow becomes simple and secure: the ARC-1 application running on SAP BTP asks the Destination Service for the SAP system details. The request is then routed through the BTP connectivity layer, passes through the SAP Cloud Connector tunnel, and finally reaches the real SAP system inside the company network. The response comes back through the same secure path.
Simple example: Cloud Connector is like a guarded private tunnel from SAP BTP to your company SAP system. Destination Service is like a contact book that tells the application which tunnel and address to use.
________________________________________
Layer 5 — How ARC-1 is deployed on SAP BTP Cloud Foundry
In this setup, each SAP system can have its own ARC-1 application running on SAP BTP Cloud Foundry. This keeps the connection, credentials, authorization, and tenant-specific behavior separate. For example, one ARC-1 application may connect to S4T, while another ARC-1 application may connect to T21.
•	arc1-mcp-s4t.cfapps.eu10-004.hana.ondemand.com → talks to S4T (client 200)
•	arc1-mcp-t21.cfapps.eu10.hana.ondemand.com → talks to T21 (client 100)
Each ARC-1 application is connected to the required BTP services, such as Connectivity and Destination. At runtime, the application reads the relevant destination name, such as S4T or T21, and uses that destination to connect securely to the correct SAP backend system. This is why the Cline configuration contains separate entries for different SAP systems.
________________________________________
Layer 6 — Your cline_mcp_settings.json block, line by line
"mcpServers": {
Cline reads this file at startup. Each key under mcpServers becomes one MCP server Cline connects to. It multiplexes tools from all of them into one tool namespace prefixed by the key (e.g. arc1_SAPRead, arc1-t21_SAPRead, ui5-mcp_run_ui5_linter).
Entry 1 — "arc1" → S4T
"arc1": {
  "type": "streamableHttp",
  "url": "https://arc1-mcp-s4t.cfapps.eu10-004.hana.ondemand.com/mcp",
•	Remote HTTP MCP server. Cline sends JSON-RPC to that URL. /mcp is the standard MCP endpoint path.
•	The hostname is a CF route in the eu10-004 landscape. That CF app is the ARC-1 server bound to the S4T destination.
  "autoApprove": [ "SAPNavigate","SAPLint","SAPDiagnose","SAPContext",
                   "SAPTransport","SAPSearch","SAPManage","SAPRead" ],
•	Trust boundary. Read-only / analysis tools Cline can call without asking you first. Anything NOT in this list (crucially SAPWrite, SAPActivate, SAPGit) still triggers a "Approve?" prompt in the Cline UI. This is the safety rail — an AI hallucination can inspect but not silently modify production.
  "disabled": false,
•	Server active. Set true to keep the config but stop connecting.
  "timeout": 300,
•	Per-tool-call max = 5 min. ADT operations like a batch activate over a big RAP stack, or a TABLE_QUERY scan of MSEG, legitimately take minutes.
  "env": {
    "NO_PROXY": "arc1-mcp-s4t.cfapps.eu10-004.hana.ondemand.com,.cfapps.eu10-004.hana.ondemand.com",
    "no_proxy": "..."
  }
}
•	BAS lives inside a corporate network that injects an HTTP proxy via HTTP_PROXY / HTTPS_PROXY env vars. If we let Cline route the ARC-1 call through that proxy, three things break:
1.	The proxy may 407 (auth required) — SSO to the corporate proxy from a background process is painful.
2.	The proxy may not support Transfer-Encoding: chunked streaming that streamableHttp uses.
3.	Latency doubles.
•	Adding the hostname to NO_PROXY tells requests / urllib3 / Node's http.Agent: "for this host, bypass the proxy, go direct." Both upper- and lowercase keys are set because Python and Node read different capitalisations.
Entry 2 — "arc1-t21" → T21
Identical shape, points at arc1-mcp-t21.cfapps.eu10.hana.ondemand.com (note: eu10, not eu10-004 — different CF landscape). This is what your grc-agent .env calls SYS_T21_ARC1_MCP_URL. Having both entries means one Cline session can query S4T and T21 in the same chat, tool names disambiguated by the server prefix.
Notice autoApprove here omits SAPSearch (vs. arc1) — likely deliberate: T21 is a smaller tenant where a wildcard TADIR search could be expensive. Config choice, not a protocol constraint.
Entry 3 — "ui5-mcp" → local Node process
"ui5-mcp": {
  "type": "stdio",
  "command": "/home/user/.asdf-inst/installs/nodejs/22.13.1/bin/node",
  "args": [ "/home/user/.node_modules_global/lib/node_modules/@ui5/mcp-server/bin/ui5mcp.js" ],
•	Different transport: stdio. Cline launches this as a subprocess. It's not a remote server. command + args = "run this Node binary with this script." The process exposes MCP over its stdin/stdout.
•	No URL, no proxy config needed — it's local.
•	Tools it provides help scaffold UI5/Fiori apps: create_ui5_app, run_ui5_linter, get_api_reference, run_manifest_validation, etc.
•	autoApprove includes everything because a linter or a scaffolder can't hurt anything beyond your local filesystem — reversible with git.
Entry 4 — "cap-mcp" → local Node process
Same pattern as ui5-mcp, but for SAP CAP (Cloud Application Programming Model). Tools: createCapProject, validateModel, getCdsCompilerMessages, search_docs, etc. Again stdio, again local, again broad autoApprove.
________________________________________
Layer 7 — The end-to-end call, once more
You in Cline chat: "On T21, find who calls FM BAPI_USER_CREATE1 and check if any Z program is broken."
1.	LLM plans: first SAPNavigate(action=references, name=BAPI_USER_CREATE1), then SAPRead on each hit, then SAPLint per program.
2.	Cline resolves SAPNavigate — sees it's in arc1-t21's autoApprove list → no prompt. Sends JSON-RPC POST to https://arc1-mcp-t21.cfapps.eu10.hana.ondemand.com/mcp.
3.	BAS OS network stack — checks NO_PROXY, sees the hostname matches, skips the corporate proxy, goes direct HTTPS.
4.	CF app arc1-mcp-t21 receives the JSON-RPC. Its handler calls destination.resolve("T21") → gets the T21 URL + BasicAuth headers.
5.	Connectivity proxy + SCC tunnel — the actual HTTP call is dispatched through the on-prem SCC daemon to the real T21 host at (say) t21.internal.corp:44300.
6.	T21 SAP system returns ADT XML for the where-used list.
7.	ARC-1 parses it → returns clean JSON { references: [...] } back over the streamed HTTP response.
8.	Cline feeds it to the LLM. LLM proceeds to step 2 (SAPRead each hit), which loops through steps 3–7 for each.
9.	On SAPLint results with fixes, LLM proposes SAPWrite — this one is not auto-approved, so Cline pauses the chat and shows you the exact ABAP diff to approve.
________________________________________
Layer 8 — Why this architecture matters (architect's TL;DR)
Concern	Answered by
Standard, model-agnostic tool protocol	MCP (works with Claude, GPT, Gemini)
Domain-specific SAP tools	ARC-1 exposes ADT surface as MCP
Per-tenant isolation	One CF app per SAP system, one destination each
No inbound firewall holes	SCC outbound tunnel + Destination Service
Read/write safety	autoApprove allow-list for read tools only
Corporate proxy compatibility	NO_PROXY per-host escape hatch
Local vs remote tools mixed cleanly	stdio + streamableHttp side-by-side
Multi-tenant chat	Server-name prefix namespaces tools
The whole point: your LLM operates as a junior SAP consultant with read-only badge access to prod and full write access to sandbox, and the badge is enforced by the MCP config, not by trusting the LLM.
________________________________________
Say the word if you want any layer expanded — e.g. hands-on "how do I build my own MCP server and deploy it to CF," or "how does Principal Propagation work vs BasicAuth for destinations," or the actual JSON-RPC wire format ARC-1 speaks.








